Here's the full **testsmells.org 19-smell academic catalog** audit of your integration test suite.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 2           | 2              |
| Medium   | 2           | 2              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4 of 8**     |

**Framework:** MSTest 4.1 · .NET 10 · Class marked `[TestCategory("Integration")]`
Integration-test calibration applied — Mystery Guest, Eager Test, and Sleepy Test severity downgraded per the taxonomy rules.

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic

**Smell:** Conditional Test Logic (testsmells.org #1)
**Test:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` — line 101
**Risk:** The `if/else` means only one branch ever executes per run. If `loaded` is never a `PremiumUser`, the `DiscountRate` assertion is dead code — a hidden testing gap.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — one branch is never exercised
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Fix — split into two tests with explicit setup:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("vip@example.com", "Vip", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("vip@example.com")!;
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**Smell:** Unknown Test / Empty Test (testsmells.org #4)
**Test:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` — line 113
**Risk:** This test passes as long as no exception is thrown. A silent data-corruption bug (e.g., only 1 of 100 rows saved, duplicates silently dropped) would never be caught.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — no assertion, false confidence
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
}
```

**Fix — assert the observable outcome:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM — Sleepy Test

**Smell:** Sleepy Test (testsmells.org #3)
**Test:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` — line 87
**Severity rationale:** Downgraded from High → Medium because this class is explicitly marked `[TestCategory("Integration")]`; async coordination delays are more understandable in integration scope. It remains a smell because `Thread.Sleep(3000)` is non-deterministic — it will be too slow on fast machines and too fast on slow CI runners.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — hard-coded 3-second sleep
Thread.Sleep(3000);
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Fix — poll with a timeout instead of sleeping:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    // Poll up to 5 s, checking every 100 ms
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow.AddSeconds(5);
    while (DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)
    {
        if (_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
            break;
        await Task.Delay(100);
    }

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        "Notification was not sent within the timeout period.");
}
```

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette

**Smell:** Assertion Roulette (testsmells.org extended catalog)
**Test:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` — lines 35-38
**Risk:** Four assertions with no message parameters. When one fails in CI, the stack trace shows a line number but no human-readable context — diagnosis requires opening the file.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — 4 bare assertions
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

**Fix — add message parameters:**

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Id should be assigned by the database");
```

---

## Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The remaining **4 tests** are well-designed:

| Test | Why it's clean |
|------|----------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Focused arrange→act→assert, 2 assertions both on-topic |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single clear assertion verifying absence |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count `3` is self-documenting (3 inserts precede it); `CollectionAssert` is appropriate |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Legitimate concurrency integration test, clear assertion |

The in-memory SQLite connection in `[TestInitialize]` is **not** a Mystery Guest — it's a hermetic, in-process database that requires no external resource. The setup fields (`_connection`, `_repository`) are used by every test, so **General Fixture** does not apply either.

---

## Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | **Assertion-Free Test** (`BulkInsert`) | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, ...)` | ~1 min |
| 2 | **Conditional Test Logic** (`GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType`) | Split into 2 deterministic tests | ~5 min |
| 3 | **Sleepy Test** (`NotifyOnInsert`) | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with poll-loop | ~10 min |
| 4 | **Assertion Roulette** (`InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase`) | Add message strings to 4 asserts | ~2 min |

Fixes 1 and 2 eliminate **false-pass risk** (tests that report green while bugs hide). Fix 3 eliminates **flakiness**. Fix 4 is quality-of-life for CI diagnostics.

**Bottom line:** Half your tests are clean and well-structured. The four findings above are real, citable smells from the academic taxonomy — fixing them (especially the assertion-free test and the conditional logic) will meaningfully improve your suite's ability to catch regressions.
